{"type":"message_update","assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"thinking_end","contentIndex":0,"content":"Now, checking what's needed:\n\n1. Containerfile and build.sh in the container/ directory. Also, container/rootfs/ contains the fn_ instrument module — but the boundary is only container/ and results/receipts/. The task says: \"COPY container/rootfs/ into site-packages so that the fn_ instrument module is placed importable.\" So, do I need to create the files in container/rootfs/? THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES says they exist under container/rootfs/ (fn_synctrace.py, fn_expert_union.py, fn_offload_batch.py) — but container/ doesn't exist in the tree yet. It's possible another task is in charge of rootfs (the instruments task), but my conflictDomains are [\"container\",\"results/receipts\"], and the COPY line has to be written by me. Since rootfs may not exist at build time, the Containerfile should COPY container/rootfs/ — podman build needs a build context that includes rootfs. I can create stub files in rootfs (is it within my boundary? container/ is within my boundary — yes, the \"container\" prefix covers container/rootfs/). However, caveat: the instruments task might also write container/rootfs/. Hmm. Per the conflictDomains writing boundary, files I modify must be inside these prefixes — and container/ is mine. But if another task owns container/rootfs, there's potential for collision. Since the brief says conflictDomains are the binding writing boundary and my domain includes all of container/, I'll write container/Containerfile, container/build.sh, and the rootfs placeholders as needed to make the build work. Actually, the safest thing is: create the fn_* module files in rootfs (in the form of retaining upstream notice headers per IMPORTS) — but that might be the job of an \"instruments\" task. Let me check silent-factory-worklists/flashnext.json and look at the task list and dependencies.\n\nAlso, \"packaging tests\": build.sh needs to \"first run the packaging tests\" — tests/test_repo_invariants.py exists. Let me read it and see whether there are packaging invariants that assert on container/. Additionally, check scripts/build-engine.sh (perhaps the nix lane's engine build) and also the worklist json regarding this task's details.\n\nFurthermore, I need the exact resolved version of triton from https://repo.amd.com/rocm/whl-multi-arch/ — I should query the index for the triton wheel matching torch 2.13.0+rocm7.14.0. If network is available, let me try curl.\n\nLet me read the worklist and tests."}}
