{"type":"turn_end","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"There's no direct description of which triton corresponds to torch 2.13.0 on the stable index. In the triton listing on the index, three cp312 wheels exist for rocm7.14.0: 3.6.0+rocm7.14.0, 3.7.1+git0263a6a6.rocm7.14.0, 3.8.0+git4cff872c.rocm7.14.0. The one with no git suffix is a stable-release triton aligned with the stable torch set. torch 2.13.0 declares triton via pytorch-triton-rocm; upstream torch 2.13 probably requires triton==3.6.0 (pytorch 2.13's third_party triton tag is… hmm, historically: torch 2.6→3.2.0, 2.7→3.3.0, 2.8→3.4.0, 2.9→3.5.0, 2.10→3.6.0? If so, 2.13 should be something newer than 3.6, but the index only publishes these for 7.14.0, and IMPORTS says \"the triton wheel accompanying the torch set on the same index\". The stable set for rocm7.14.0 is accompanied by triton-3.6.0+rocm7.14.0 (with the git-suffixed ones being TheRock-based builds).\n\nActually, one more consideration: kyuz0's auto-detection workflow iterates over torch from newest to oldest and looks for \"a complete aligned set\" — for rocm7.14.0, it resolves torch 2.11.0 (because of torchaudio's upper bound). The tag there doesn't tell us about triton.\n\nLet me check the wheel listing more carefully: the index root's simple page might list dependencies. Or check AMD's documentation page about the whl-multi-arch repo. Try: curl the index root.\n\nOr check torch 2.13.0's metadata via PyPI? No — +rocm wheels aren't on PyPI.\n\nAnother approach: pip dry-run resolution. `pip install --dry-run --ignore-installed --index-url ... torch==2.13.0+rocm7.14.0` would need to download wheel metadata — the index doesn't serve .metadata, so pip downloads the whole wheel (which gets blocked anyway).\n\nI'll go with triton==3.6.0+rocm7.14.0 and write in a comment: \"resolved from the stable index as the triton accompanying the torch 2.13.0+rocm7.14.0 set (the only non-git-suffixed rocm7.14.0 triton wheel on the index; satisfies the triton < 3.8 gate)\". Also, have the Containerfile capture the actually-installed triton version into build-info.json, and have build.sh read it into the receipt. If pip resolution disagrees at build time, the receipt will record the truth, but… the strict pin `triton==3.6.0+rocm7.14.0` would fail if torch requires a different version. To be safe, should I explicitly install triton first and then install torch with --no-deps? No — relaxing is not allowed, but the triton pin has to be the exact one. IMPORTS says \"record the exact resolved triton version\".\n\nHmm, one more double-check: check the index root's simple page for a \"triton\" package name; maybe there's also \"pytorch-triton-rocm\" for other rocm versions. The earlier grep found nothing about pytorch-triton-rocm. Also check triton for rocm7.13.0: 3.4.0 and 3.6.0. The stable-aligned triton for 7.14.0 is 3.6.0. OK.\n\nJust in case, verify torch 2.13 → triton 3.6 on upstream PyPI: torch 2.13.0 on PyPI requires triton==3.6.0. PyTorch 2.13 (release around November 2026?). Upstream mapping I remember: torch 2.10 → triton 3.6.0? Hmm. pytorch's .ci/docker/ci_commit_pins/triton.txt: 2.9 → 3.5. If it's one minor bump per release: 2.10→3.5? Actually, torch 2.9 was released with triton 3.5.0; 2.10 with 3.6.0… If 2.13 → 3.6.0 or later, hmm, but AMD's stable index only has 3.6.0 aligned to the rocm7.14.0 release. AMD's stable torch 2.13.0+rocm7.14.0 wheel would declare the triton that ships with that set, i.e., pytorch-triton-rocm 3.6.0+rocm7.14.0 — which exists on the index as `triton-3.6.0+rocm7.14.0`. This is reasonable.\n\nQuick sanity check via PyPI JSON for torch 2.13.0's requires (the CPU wheel's metadata indicates the triton pin):","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"text","text":"The exact triton needs verification. Let me check upstream torch 2.13's triton pin via PyPI metadata as a cross-check.\n\n"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_17bf78b79aa44947a32b43a2","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"curl -fsSL --max-time 30 https://pypi.org/pypi/torch/2.13.0/json 2>/dev/null | python3 -c \"import json,sys; d=json.load(sys.stdin); print([r for r in d['info']['requires_dist'] if 'triton' in r.lower()])\" 2>/dev/null || echo \"pypi torch 2.13.0 not found\"; curl -fsSL --max-time 30 \"https://repo.amd.com/rocm/whl-multi-arch/\" | grep -oE 'href=\"[^\"]+/\"' | head -40","timeout":90}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"qwen-token-plan","model":"qwen3.8-max","usage":{"input":2717,"output":1416,"cacheRead":46464,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":1206,"totalTokens":50597,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1787983686871,"responseId":"chatcmpl-c8119546-8928-4d20-b714-5f102be1bc98","rawStopReason":"tool_calls"},"toolResults":[{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_17bf78b79aa44947a32b43a2","toolName":"bash","content":[{"type":"text","text":"['triton==3.7.1; platform_system == \"Linux\" and python_version < \"3.15\"']\nhref=\"amd-torch-device-gfx1010/\"\nhref=\"amd-torch-device-gfx1011/\"\nhref=\"amd-torch-device-gfx1012/\"\nhref=\"amd-torch-device-gfx1030/\"\nhref=\"amd-torch-device-gfx1031/\"\nhref=\"amd-torch-device-gfx1032/\"\nhref=\"amd-torch-device-gfx1033/\"\nhref=\"amd-torch-device-gfx1034/\"\nhref=\"amd-torch-device-gfx1035/\"\nhref=\"amd-torch-device-gfx1036/\"\nhref=\"amd-torch-device-gfx11/\"\nhref=\"amd-torch-device-gfx1100/\"\nhref=\"amd-torch-device-gfx1101/\"\nhref=\"amd-torch-device-gfx1102/\"\nhref=\"amd-torch-device-gfx1103/\"\nhref=\"amd-torch-device-gfx110x/\"\nhref=\"amd-torch-device-gfx1150/\"\nhref=\"amd-torch-device-gfx1151/\"\nhref=\"amd-torch-device-gfx1152/\"\nhref=\"amd-torch-device-gfx1153/\"\nhref=\"amd-torch-device-gfx115x/\"\nhref=\"amd-torch-device-gfx1200/\"\nhref=\"amd-torch-device-gfx1201/\"\nhref=\"amd-torch-device-gfx1250/\"\nhref=\"amd-torch-device-gfx12-0/\"\nhref=\"amd-torch-device-gfx908/\"\nhref=\"amd-torch-device-gfx90a/\"\nhref=\"amd-tor