{"type":"message_update","assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"thinking_end","contentIndex":0,"content":"Upstream torch 2.13.0 declares `triton==3.7.1`. And the AMD stable index has `triton-3.7.1+git0263a6a6.rocm7.14.0-cp312` — this is the ROCm build of the triton 3.7.1 that torch 2.13.0 declares (IMPORTS fallback: \"the pytorch-triton-rocm wheel declared by torch 2.13.0\"). So the corresponding triton is `triton==3.7.1+git0263a6a6.rocm7.14.0`. This is still below 3.8.0 ✓.\n\nWait — but does the ROCm torch wheel declare `pytorch-triton-rocm` rather than `triton`? The ROCm torch wheel usually requires `pytorch-triton-rocm==3.7.1+git0263a6a6.rocm7.14.0`. That package name doesn't exist on the index (the grep found nothing), but `triton` does exist under that same version string. kyuz0's workflow resolves triton from the same index. Therefore pinning `triton==3.7.1+git0263a6a6.rocm7.14.0` from the vendor's index is correct, and it also matches torch's declaration (version 3.7.1, commit 0263a6a6 — note: interestingly, nix-strix-halo pins triton rev 0263a6a6 in TheRock 7.15/torch 2.11, so 0263a6a6 is the triton commit that AMD builds for its stable series).\n\nTo make the pinning robust: install triton explicitly at that pin, and install torch with a --no-deps-ish approach? If the ROCm torch wheel's Requires-Dist is `pytorch-triton-rocm==...` and that doesn't exist on the index, then installing torch with deps from that index will fail to resolve triton. kyuz0's approach: install torch and triton explicitly. Best practice: either pip install both wheels from the index with `--no-deps` to install the set, or install triton first and then install torch with the constraint that pytorch-triton-rocm is provided by… but pip can't alias. The standard AMD approach (kyuz0's Dockerfile.ubuntu-repoamd) probably does:\n\npip install torch==... torchvision==... triton==... --index-url https://repo.amd.com/rocm/whl-multi-arch\n\nIf torch's metadata requires pytorch-triton-rocm, pip will fail. Actually, do AMD's ROCm wheels declare the requirement `pytorch-triton-rocm==3.7.1+git0263a6a6.rocm7.14.0; platform_system == 'Linux'`? Let me check the amd-torch-device-gfx1151 package on the index — maybe that's a meta wheel. Hmm.\n\nSince I can't download the wheel from this host to check, the safer approach is: install the three wheels with `pip install --index-url ... torch==2.13.0+rocm7.14.0 torchvision==0.28.0+rocm7.14.0 triton==3.7.1+git0263a6a6.rocm7.14.0` and pass `--no-deps` for safety? No — --no-deps would skip the torch dependency _rocm_sdk_core… wait, per the evidence the ROCm userspace is included in the torch wheel set (`_rocm_sdk_core`). That probably also comes from the same index as a wheel (amd-torch-device-gfx1151 might be a meta package that pulls in _rocm_sdk_core_gfx1151). Actually, the listing of `amd-torch-device-gfx1151/` in the evidence suggests a meta package for the device set.\n\nkyuz0's workflow resolution is: iterate over torch from newest; install torch+torchvision+torchaudio+triton as the aligned set from the index. Dockerfile.ubuntu-repoamd:66-67 has ARG ROCM_VERSION and installs wheels. The evidence §3.4 cited: \"the whole gfx1151 ROCm/torch stack is STABLE release wheels from https://repo.amd.com/rocm/whl-multi-arch/ … torch brings its own matched ROCm (_rocm_sdk_core)\". So torch's own deps include _rocm_sdk_core*, and pip resolves them from the same index (they must exist there: probably a `_rocm-sdk-core` package).\n\nPlan: follow kyuz0's form: single pip install command using --index-url with the pinned trio (explicitly install triton first to satisfy torch's triton requirement if the name matches; if the name is pytorch-triton-rocm, the build will fail at the cp-build checkpoint and surface on the overnight ladder — but I should minimize that risk).\n\nA robust trick many ROCm container builds use: install the wheels directly by URL with --no-deps, then install remaining deps from PyPI. But the torch wheel's runtime dep _rocm_sdk_core is needed. Hmm, but the actual kyuz0 Dockerfile is available… no wait, there's no local clone here.\n\nLet me look for _rocm-sdk-core and pytorch-triton-rocm in the index root listing."}}
