{"type":"message_update","assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"thinking_end","contentIndex":0,"content":"Podman available, 114G free disk, 32 cores, 125G RAM, GPU present. Full build possible but torch wheel ~2-3GB + vLLM build (~1-2h on 32 cores?); cp-build's runtimeMaxSec is 14400 (4h). If it fits in session, trying the build here is valuable. The brief says commit the result on the assigned branch; the build receipt is written by cp-build checkpoint (a campaign node), but my build.sh writes it at runtime. Deliverables are recipe + committed files. Actually running the build validates everything. But if the session ends early, a partial build doesn't hurt (artifacts go to .build/ or podman storage, uncommitted; receipts: hmm — if I run build.sh here, results/receipts/build.json gets written and is within my write boundary, and receipts-verify would score it. Claim 3.1 expects the receipt to record pass along with lane/torch/triton/wallclock. If my build succeeds, committing that receipt is actually a delivery of claim 3.1. If it fails mid-way, don't commit the fail receipt (or commit with status fail? no — better not commit fail; but build.sh writes to results/receipts/build.json anyway... delete if fails, or just don't commit it).\n\nPlan:\n1. Write container/Containerfile, container/build.sh, container/rootfs/.keep.\n2. Verify acceptance criteria commands pass.\n3. Attempt full build via container/build.sh (may take 1-3h). Monitor.\n4. Commit on assigned branch: container/ files (+ build.json if pass).\n5. Report.\n\nOne consideration about packaging tests: build.sh runs `python3 -m unittest discover -s tests` — currently the tests directory only has test_repo_invariants.py (instruments/host tests don't exist yet). Discover finds that; fine. But note test_repo_invariants.test_receipts_verify_runs_clean — receipts currently pass. After writing build.json with pass, it'll pass too.\n\nAlso \"run packaging tests first\" — cp-build's runtimeMaxSec=14400 includes tests + build.\n\nWriting the Containerfile. Ubuntu 24.04 specifics: python3.12 is the default python3 in noble. Packages: python3.12-venv (ensurepip), python3.12-dev, git, build-essential, cmake, ninja-build, ccache, curl, ca-certificates.\n\nvLLM build requirements (no-build-isolation): setuptools, wheel, packaging, cmake (apt), ninja (apt), torch (present). pyproject may require `setuptools-scm` (does vLLM use it? vLLM uses setuptools>=61; version via _version.py... does vLLM pyproject have [build-system] requires: \"setuptools>=61\", \"wheel\", \"cmake>=3.26\", \"ninja\", \"packaging\", \"setuptools-scm>=8.0\"? I recall recent vLLM uses setuptools-scm for version. To be safe, pip install setuptools wheel packaging setuptools-scm (from PyPI with comments).\n\nAlso vLLM runtime deps via pip install -e . will pull a big dep tree (ray, transformers, torch already satisfied, xformers? ROCm path skips xformers; flashinfer? ROCm skips). Some deps may try to pull CUDA-only wheels (e.g. `nvidia-*` pulled by torch on PyPI — our torch is from vendor index with +rocm local version; dep resolution: torch==2.13.0+rocm7.14.0 already installed → vLLM's requirement `torch>=...` satisfied by installed, no re-fetch. Good.\n\nPotential issue: `pip install -e .` on this branch may try to fetch nightly index or extras like vllm-flash-attn? ROCm uses composable_kernel/triton attention; recent vLLM ROCm builds use triton attention by default. OK.\n\nENV for vLLM ROCm source build:\n- VLLM_TARGET_DEVICE=rocm\n- PYTORCH_ROCM_ARCH=gfx1151\n- MAX_JOBS for control.\n\nccache: HIP compilation via hipcc/clang — ccache works with CCACHE_* and CMAKE_CXX_COMPILER_LAUNCHER. Does vLLM's cmake pick up ccache? If on PATH, it auto-detects ccache via find_program? vLLM's CMakeLists has compiler launcher logic (USE_CCACHE?). Does vLLM support `VLLM_USE_CCACHE`? Unclear. Newer vLLM: there's cmake logic for ccache detection: yes, vLLM's CMakeLists.txt contains `find_program(CCACHE_PROGRAM ccache)` and sets CMAKE_<LANG>_COMPILER_LAUNCHER (it exists in upstream vLLM, used in ROCm CI). So putting ccache on PATH + CCACHE_DIR is enough.\n\nNow, the bind-mount iteration story inside build.sh:\n- `FN_CCACHE_DIR=\"${FN_CCACHE_DIR:-$REPO_ROOT/.build/ccache}\"` mounted to /root/.ccache with `-v`.\n- `FN_VLLM_SRC` optional host fork checkout mounted to /opt/vllm; Containerfile guards clone with `[ -d /opt/vllm/.git ] ||`.\n- Build dir persistence: with source mounted, editable build artifacts under /opt/vllm persist on host. If not mounted, RUN --mount=type=cache,target=/opt/vllm/.build-cache? Not necessary.\n\nAlso to support edit-and-resume iteration without rebuild: keep the venv + tools in the image and document `podman run -it -v src:/opt/vllm flashnext:dev bash` then `pip install -e . --no-build-isolation`. Keep build tools in final image (single stage).\n\nWORKDIR /opt/vllm, ENV HOME=/root.\n\nbuild-info.json via python script inside RUN:\n\nRUN set -eux; mkdir -p /opt/flashnext; FORK_COMMIT=$(git -C /opt/vllm rev-parse HEAD); \\\npython3 -c 'import json,time,importlib.metadata as md; ... write /opt/flashnext/build-info.json with fork_commit, torch, triton, torchvision, built_at, branch'\n\nNote: python3 in PATH = the one from venv (due to ENV PATH). Good — importlib.metadata sees torch/triton.\n\nLayer ordering for iteration efficiency: substrate wheels first (rarely change), then clone+build (often), then rootfs COPY (often), then build-info. But build-info needs fork commit — write it in the build RUN, then add build timestamp after rootfs copy? Simpler: write build-info.json in the last RUN after COPY rootfs (RUN --mount=cache ... python writes json). COPY rootfs layer must come before that. OK.\n\nAlso image needs `ENTRYPOINT`/`CMD`: CMD [\"bash\"]? kyuz0 uses vllm serve as CMD? For iteration, CMD [\"bash\"] is fine; smoke script overrides with `python3 -`. run-smoke.sh runs `podman run ... \"$IMAGE\" python3 -` → CMD becomes python3 args. CMD [\"bash\"] works.\n\nENV defaults that the engine reads via is-set probes are forbidden (F.9) — don't set FN_* envs in the image. Set VLLM_PLE_MMAP in image? That's a runtime env belonging to the host/fn-env.sh. Don't set it.\n\nNow writing build.sh in the style of run-smoke.sh: set -euo pipefail, REPO_ROOT, IMAGE, OUT, receipt writing function with status fail trap.\n\nReceipt schema: {\"step\":\"build\",\"status\":\"pass\",\"ts\":...,\"data\":{\"lane\":\"container\",\"torch\":...,\"triton\":...,\"fork_commit\":...,\"image_digest\":...,\"wall_clock_s\":...}}. Spec 3.1 wants lane, torch, triton, wall-clock — fork_commit and image_digest are required per task goal.\n\nTrap: on ERR, write fail receipt with captured partial data, exit nonzero.\n\nDigest: `podman inspect --format '{{.Digest}}' \"$IMAGE\"` — often empty for local builds; fallback to `{{.Id}}`. Do DIGEST=$(podman inspect ... '.Digest'); [ -z ] → .Id.\n\nAlso record image tag.\n\nEdge case: `podman build --timestamp` for reproducibility? \"Reproducible image build\" — mainly refers to pinned versions. Optionally could add --timestamp $(date +%s)... skip.\n\nMAX_JOBS default: nproc.\n\nWriting files now. Also `.keep`: build.sh does `mkdir -p container/rootfs; touch container/rootfs/.keep`? If .keep exists, commit it (so the Containerfile COPY works without build.sh prep). Yes, commit container/rootfs/.keep. But wait — could COPY container/rootfs/ include .keep into site-packages → harmless.\n\nAlso Containerfile must have the line \"COPY container/rootfs/ /opt/venv/lib/python3.12/site-packages/\". Build context = repo root.\n\nNote: site-packages path python3.12 — Ubuntu noble venv's python is 3.12 → /opt/venv/lib/python3.12/site-packages. To be robust, compute in RUN and use a shell cp instead of COPY? COPY doesn't do dynamic targets. Fine to hardcode python3.12 (acceptance criteria imply this).\n\nOne more point: Containerfile acceptance criteria greps for 'mecattaf/vllm' ✓ (clone URL), 'repo.amd.com/rocm/whl-multi-arch' ✓, 'torch==2.13.0+rocm7.14.0' ✓, MIT ✓ (case-insensitive). build.sh greps 'flashnext:dev' ✓, bash -n ✓.\n\nTriton pin note: IMPORTS says \"record the exact triton version resolved\" — record 3.7.1+git0263a6a6.rocm7.14.0 as comment + pinned install + receipt.\n\nConfirm triton-3.7.1+git0263a6a6.rocm7.14.0-cp312 wheel exists on index ✓ (saw it earlier).\n\nWriting Containerfile:\n\nNote on --mount=type=cache support: NixOS podman version? Quick check: podman --version. buildah cache mounts require buildah >= 1.29 / podman >= 4.4 or so. Should be fine; but as a safety, if not supported the build fails. Guard: rely on build.sh's -v mounts for ccache (works everywhere) instead of RUN --mount cache, and use RUN --mount=type=cache only for pip? For maximum portability, avoid RUN --mount entirely: instead persist caches via the bind-mount that build.sh passes (podman build -v). In bare podman build (no mounts) pip cache just doesn't persist — acceptable. ccache likewise via -v. Simpler, fewer failure modes. CCACHE_DIR=/root/.ccache so -v hostdir:/root/.ccache lines up.\n\nBut note: podman build -v writes to host dir with root uid (rootless → subuid mapping, files owned by subuid user; fix permissions later — tolerable for cache dirs under .build/).\n\nWriting everything now."}}
{"type":"message_update","assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"text_end","contentIndex":1,"content":"Host can build: podman, 32 cores, 125G RAM, 114G free, GPU present. Now I'll write the recipe files.\n\n"}}
{"type":"message_update","assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"toolcall_end","contentIndex":2,"toolCall":{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_b28cdf2ac2ac4a8288bdf94f","name":"write","arguments":{"path":"/home/tom/.local/state/tally/campaigns/flashnext/flashnext/824b68c09c23/container-recipe/container/Containerfile","content":"# flashnext:dev — the engine fork built from source at the exactly pinned wheel set\n# ============================================================================\n#\n# Recipe shape adapted from kyuz0/amd-strix-halo-vllm-toolboxes,\n# `Dockerfile.ubuntu-repoamd` @ 23cb726, credited per THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md.\n# That recipe is MIT licensed; the notice is retained here as required:\n#\n#   MIT License — Copyright (c) kyuz0 (amd-strix-halo-vllm-toolboxes)\n#\n#   Permission is hereby granted, free of charge, to any person obtaining a\n#   copy of this software and associated documentation files (the \"Software\"),\n#   to deal in the Software without restriction, including without limitation\n#   the rights to use, copy, modify, merge, publish, distribute, sublicense,\n#   and/or sell copies of the Software, and to permit persons to whom the\n#   Software is furnished to do so, subject to the following conditions:\n#\n#   The above copyright notice and this permission notice shall be included in\n#   all copies or substantial portions of the Software.\n#\n#   THE SOFTWARE IS PROVIDED \"AS IS\", WITHOUT WARRANTY OF ANY KIND, EXPRESS OR\n#   IMPLIED, INCLUDING BUT NOT LIMITED TO THE WARRANTIES OF MERCHANTABILITY,\n#   FITNESS FOR A PARTICULAR PURPOSE AND NONINFRINGEMENT. IN NO EVENT SHALL THE\n#   AUTHORS OR COPYRIGHT HOLDERS BE LIABLE FOR ANY CLAIM, DAMAGES OR OTHER\n#   LIABILITY, WHETHER IN AN ACTION OF CONTRACT, TORT OR OTHERWISE, ARISING\n#   FROM, OUT OF OR IN CONNECTION WITH THE SOFTWARE OR THE USE OR OTHER\n#   DEALINGS IN THE SOFTWARE.\n#\n# What changed vs the donor recipe (per IMPORTS.md sections 1 and 2):\n#   - torch is pinned to the fork's exact pin 2.13.0+rocm7.14.0 from the AMD\n#     stable multi-arch index (the index tops out there; no ROCm 10 wheels\n#     exist for gfx1151 — specs/flashnext/evidence/kyuz0-rocm10.md).\n#   - torchaudio is OMITTED; we drop the audio extra rather than downgrade\n#     torch to 2.11 (torchaudio capped kyuz0's auto-resolved set there).\n#   - the engine is the flashnext fork (mecattaf/vllm, branch flashnext),\n#     built from source for gfx1151, not a stock vLLM ref.\n#   - designed for overnight iteration: bind-mount a fork checkout over\n#     /opt/vllm and a host dir over /root/.ccache (see container/build.sh);\n#     the editable install then recompiles only what a one-line patch touched.\n#\n# Wheel provenance: the torch/triton/vision/rocm-sdk substrate comes ONLY\n# from the vendor stable index (spec F.11). Generic Python build tooling\n# (setuptools, wheel, packaging, setuptools-scm) and vLLM's upstream runtime\n# dependencies resolve from PyPI as upstream vLLM does; none of those is part\n# of the pinned substrate.\n\nFROM docker.io/ubuntu:24.04\n\nARG ROCM_WHL=https://repo.amd.com/rocm/whl-multi-arch/\n# The exactly pinned wheel set (IMPORTS.md section 1). The triton pin is the\n# wheel accompanying the torch 2.13.0 set on the vendor stable index — the\n# ROCm build of the triton 3.7.1 that torch 2.13.0 declares (git0263a6a6),\n# and it sits under the triton < 3.8 gate the fork's fp8-upcast keys on.\nARG TORCH_PIN=\"torch==2.13.0+rocm7.14.0\"\nARG TORCHVISION_PIN=\"torchvision==0.28.0+rocm7.14.0\"\nARG TRITON_PIN=\"triton==3.7.1+git0263a6a6.rocm7.14.0\"\n\nENV DEBIAN_FRONTEND=noninteractive\nRUN apt-get update && apt-get install -y --no-install-recommends \\\n        python3.12 python3.12-venv python3.12-dev \\\n        git ca-certificates curl \\\n        build-essential cmake ninja-build ccache \\\n    && rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/*\n\n# Python 3.12 venv at /opt/venv (pip arrives via ensurepip — no external fetch).\nRUN python3.12 -m venv /opt/venv\nENV VIRTUAL_ENV=/opt/venv \\\n    PATH=/opt/venv/bin:$PATH \\\n    PIP_NO_CACHE_DIR=0 \\\n    PIP_DISABLE_PIP_VERSION_CHECK=1 \\\n    CCACHE_DIR=/root/.ccache \\\n    HOME=/root\n\n# Python build tooling (setuptools is served by the vendor index itself; the\n# rest is generic build-time tooling, not substrate).\nRUN pip install --index-url ${ROCM_WHL} --extra-index-url https://pypi.org/simple \\\n        setuptools wheel packaging \"setuptools-scm>=8.0\"\n\n# ---- the pinned wheel substrate (vendor stable index only, spec F.11) ----\nRUN pip install --index-url ${ROCM_WHL} \\\n        ${TORCH_PIN} ${TORCHVISION_PIN} ${TRITON_PIN}\n\n# ---- the engine fork, built from source for gfx1151 ----\nARG VLLM_REPO=https://github.com/mecattaf/vllm\nARG VLLM_BRANCH=flashnext\nENV VLLM_TARGET_DEVICE=rocm \\\n    PYTORCH_ROCM_ARCH=gfx1151\n\n# If a host fork checkout is bind-mounted over /opt/vllm (build.sh -v), keep\n# it — that is the incremental-iteration path. Otherwise clone the baseline.\nRUN if [ ! -d /opt/vllm/.git ]; then \\\n        git clone --depth 1 --branch flashnext https://github.com/mecattaf/vllm /opt/vllm; \\\n    fi \\\n    && git -C /opt/vllm rev-parse HEAD > /opt/flashnext-fork-commit \\\n    && git -C /opt/vllm rev-parse --abbrev-ref HEAD >> /opt/flashnext-fork-commit\n\n# Editable, no build isolation: the build links against the venv's pinned\n# torch and never resolves build-system.requires (the fork's torch==2.11.0\n# build-system pin is therefore irrelevant here — IMPORTS.md section 1).\n# Build artifacts land inside /opt/vllm, so a bind-mounted checkout keeps\n# them across rebuilds and ccache (on PATH, auto-detected by vLLM's cmake)\n# recompiles only what changed.\nRUN pip install -e /opt/vllm --no-build-isolation\n\n# ---- the fn_ instrument overlay ----\n# Lands the instrument modules importable at the top level of site-packages.\nCOPY container/rootfs/ /opt/venv/lib/python3.12/site-packages/\n\n# ---- build identity ----\nRUN mkdir -p /opt/flashnext && python3 - <<'PY' \\\n    && cat /opt/flashnext/build-info.json\nimport json, time, importlib.metadata as md\ninfo = {\n    \"lane\": \"container\",\n    \"image\": \"flashnext:dev\",\n    \"fork_repo\": \"https://github.com/mecattaf/vllm\",\n    \"fork_branch\": open(\"/opt/flashnext-fork-commit\").read().splitlines()[1],\n    \"fork_commit\": open(\"/opt/flashnext-fork-commit\").read().splitlines()[0],\n    \"torch\": md.version(\"torch\"),\n    \"torchvision\": md.version(\"torchvision\"),\n    \"triton\": md.version(\"triton\"),\n    \"rocm_sdk_core\": md.version(\"rocm-sdk-core\"),\n    \"gpu_target\": \"gfx1151\",\n    \"built_at\": time.strftime(\"%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%S%z\"),\n}\njson.dump(info, open(\"/opt/flashnext/build-info.json\", \"w\"), indent=1)\nPY\n\nWORKDIR /opt/vllm\nCMD [\"bash\"]\n"}}}}
