{"type":"turn_end","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Progress: Substrate installed cleanly including triton 3.8.0+git4cff872c and rocm-sdk-core. The fork's setup.py (line 21) imports `setuptools_rust` (rocSHMEM? — upstream vLLM recently added a rocSHMEM build option that uses rust? Actually \"E-001h rocSHMEM Could not open libnuma\" — vLLM is trying to build rocshmem… does the latest vLLM include a rocshmem submodule that requires setuptools_rust? Hmm, the vllm ROCm path might be building `vllm_flash_attn` or rocSHMEM). Fix: pip install setuptools-rust (PyPI, build tooling), and libnuma via apt (libnuma-dev). The rocSHMEM log line is a warning from the build helper.\n\nAdd `setuptools-rust` to the build tooling pip line and add `libnuma-dev`? The \"Could not open libnuma\" was emitted by some probe during setup import — probably from a prebuilt rocshmem binary being run during setup. Installing libnuma1/libnuma-dev would be good. Also the Rust toolchain may be needed for setuptools-rust to actually compile something — if the fork builds a rust extension without cargo, it'll fail. setuptools_rust is imported at line 21 of setup.py — probably just to declare an optional extension. Let's see: add setuptools-rust and check.\n\nNote: apt layer changes will partially invalidate the cache (the apt layer comes before the venv; the substrate layer is after → rebuild of apt+venv+tools is fast since the cache is local? apt layer change → everything from that point rebuilds, but the wheel downloads are only cached by the pip cache if it persists — PIP_NO_CACHE_DIR=0 and the default cache /root/.cache/pip inside the container is not persisted across builds (no cache mount). So a full re-download of the wheels (~6GB). Hmm. To avoid the re-download, add a pip cache bind mount via build.sh (-v $REPO_ROOT/.build/pip-cache:/root/.cache/pip). podman build -v works. Let's add this to BUILD_ARGS. Also, should we set PIP_NO_CACHE_DIR=0? pip uses the cache directory by default; the env var PIP_NO_CACHE_DIR=0 means the cache is enabled (no-cache disabled). OK.\n\nAdd to build.sh: --volume \"$PIP_CACHE:/root/.cache/pip\", default $REPO_ROOT/.build/pip-cache.\n\nContainerfile changes:\n- apt: add libnuma-dev (and probably libnuma1).\n- pip tooling line: add setuptools-rust.\n\nAnd, the root cause of the rocSHMEM numa warning: install libnuma1 via apt.\n\nAlso, the cargo possibility: setuptools_rust is only imported at the top of setup.py (line 21) — upstream vLLM added `from setuptools_rust import ...` for building the `vllm._moe` rust extension? Recent upstream added a rust-based sampler (the \"sampler in Rust\" work — yes, vLLM has been migrating the sampler to Rust, and setuptools-rust is in the build requires). The fork's pyproject [build-system].requires probably lists setuptools-rust; with --no-build-isolation we need to provide it, plus cargo/rustc via apt? apt's rustc is somewhat old; the rust extension build needs cargo+rustc. Ubuntu 24.04's rustc is 1.75 — might be sufficient. Should I add `cargo rustc` via apt? If the fork builds the rust extension unconditionally… upstream vLLM 0.28+: yes, it's building a rust-based sampling module (\"vllm._sampling\"?) hmm. Add cargo + rustc to the apt list, and if it's too slow or the wrong version the build will tell us.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"text","text":"Substrate installed cleanly. The fork's setup needs `setuptools_rust` (and numa). I'll add those plus a persisted pip cache mount to avoid re-downloading wheels:\n\n"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_459985c1823c44d1b57c7e68","name":"edit","arguments":{"edits":[{"newText":"        python3.12 python3.12-venv python3.12-dev \\\n        git ca-certificates curl \\\n        build-essential cmake ninja-build ccache \\\n        cargo rustc libnuma-dev \\","oldText":"        python3.12 python3.12-venv python3.12-dev \\\n        git ca-certificates curl \\\n        build-essential cmake ninja-bui