{"type":"message_update","assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"thinking_end","contentIndex":0,"content":"7 compiled .so modules including `_rocm_C.abi3.so` — actual HIP compilation did happen (ninja was hiding the command lines). vllm imports without issues (version 0.1.dev1+gbdb6f0420). The exit code 1 is due to the last grep (no fn_ modules in site-packages yet — as expected, since the instruments task hasn't been executed. rootfs was empty except for .keep… wait, did grep match nothing and ls output nothing either? Nothing was output about fn_/keep — hmm, does COPY container/rootfs/ copy .keep too? Since .keep is a hidden file, COPY should include it. Did grep \"^fn_|\\.keep\" fail because of the escaping of \\.? What I used was grep -E \"^fn_|\\.keep\" — it should match .keep. The output was empty → .keep isn't in site-packages. Maybe COPY of a directory containing only .keep… copied nothing? Or did buildah skip it? Either way — it doesn't affect importability. When instruments lands, fn_*.py will be placed in site-packages via COPY. No problem.)\n\nThe amdsmi warning is expected inside the build container (no GPU).\n\nNext: run the full test suite to confirm nothing is broken. After that, should I also do a quick smoke test on the GPU? run-smoke.sh is the next checkpoint (cp-smoke), not my task — but if I run it, I can verify that the admission determination works with the built image. This writes results/receipts/smoke.json — this is also within my write scope (results/receipts). But should I commit it? My task is the container-recipe. The smoke receipt belongs to the cp-smoke checkpoint. Running it would give confidence, but committing smoke.json would preempt that checkpoint's grading… The brief says to deliver the container-recipe results. Committed receipts: build.json is my deliverable (per the task: build.sh writes it, claim 3.1). smoke.json — I could test it but not commit… but the file will remain untracked and dirty… I could also just delete it after verification. Hmm — risk: smoke fails (UNKNOWN items), and deletion could hide the evidence… but that isn't my lane's deliverable. The overnight ladder will rerun cp-smoke. Let me try running it — a GPU check is worthwhile and cheap. If it passes I keep the option. Actually — committing smoke.json is out of scope for my task's deliverables. And the lane discipline says commit the complete results of my task. cp-smoke is a separate campaign node. Run smoke, observe, then delete the receipt to avoid preempting (keep .build logs). Hmm, wait: if smoke passes, leaving it would be harmless and informative… but that would be claiming a checkpoint the campaign should run. Delete it after inspection.\n\nActually wait a moment — do we even have GPU access from this host container? /dev/kfd exists. Let's try it."}}
