{"type":"message_update","assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"thinking_end","contentIndex":0,"content":"The container is outputting nothing, and yet it exits with exit code 0! Even the `print(\"hello\")` is being suppressed. Something is eating up stdout. Is stdin `-` causing the problem? With `python3 -`, the script gets read from stdin... In the case of a heredoc: podman run without -i? By default, if not a TTY, podman run keeps stdin attached, right? `python3 -` needs stdin. podman run without `-i` might not forward stdin → python reads EOF → empty script → exit code 0, no output! run-smoke.sh has the same problem too — that script is existing estate (not mine to modify — the scripts/ boundary doesn't include run-smoke.sh). Does it need `-i`? Actually, does `podman run` keep stdin open by default? Docker requires -i for non-TTY stdin. Same for Podman. Which means run-smoke.sh as written has that latent bug... or does it work in the campaign node's podman? The default is the same. Hmm, but wait — maybe it works in the pipeline: a heredoc to podman's stdin... podman run without --interactive doesn't attach stdin. Right, that's it.\n\nBut scripts/ is outside my write boundary (\"touch only container/ and results/receipts/\"). Can't fix run-smoke.sh. For my own validation, I'll re-run smoke inline with -i. This is just a local probe, not a deliverable. The cp-smoke checkpoint has the same bug... but that's for the campaign to deal with (maybe their podman version behaves differently, or maybe the judge notices it). Hmm — should I report it? I could mention it in my summary. It's outside my authority to modify.\n\nAlright, let me try the smoke probe with -i."}}
