{"type":"turn_end","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Even a simple float32 kernel fails with hipErrorInvalidImage. The device is detected as gfx1151, and total_memory is 131072MB. In other words, the kernels bundled with the torch wheel don't match this GPU's runtime — probably a kernel driver/userspace mismatch. The host's kernel is on ROCm 7.1.4 driver, and IMPORTS says rocm 7.14.0 userspace \"matches the kernel's 7.1.4 driver on both nodes.\" But this host might have a different kernel/driver, or might need an environment variable like HSA_OVERRIDE_GFX_VERSION=11.5.1 (gfx1151 often requires HSA_OVERRIDE_GFX_VERSION=11.5.0/11.5.1 since the runtime only knows about gfx1150?). For Strix Halo, isn't the known HSA_OVERRIDE_GFX_VERSION 11.5.1? On Strix Point (gfx1150), people use HSA_OVERRIDE_GFX_VERSION=11.5.0. On Strix Halo (gfx1151), the override is also 11.5.1... hmm, wait — but the AMD-provided gfx1151 wheels are supposed to work natively.\n\nWait a sec — is this host really a \"coordinator\" node with the proper amdgpu stack? Check the host's ROCm kernel version: `modinfo amdgpu | grep version` or cat /sys/module/amdgpu/version, and whether a host-level torch works. But hold on: the cp-smoke checkpoint is supposed to run on this estate, and the evidence says the nodes have been validated (the \"2026-08-28 verified\" index is gfx1151-capable). kyuz0's toolboxes run on this fleet...\n\nLet me check the host: does host-level torch with the same wheels work here? Also check for missing environment variables. Try HSA_OVERRIDE_GFX_VERSION=11.5.1:\n\nAlso, hipErrorInvalidImage can occur when the runtime rejects an ISA it doesn't know about: the host kernel's KFD might be reporting gfx1151, but the HSA runtime inside the container might not support it... the rocm-sdk-core 7.14 userspace definitely knows gfx1151.\n\nActually, wait: \"device kernel image is invalid\" often happens when the amdgpu kernel module is older than required by the ISA, or when the code object targets a different gfx. The torch wheels are... the wheel's torch ships binaries compiled for a set of architectures (multi-arch: gfx90x, gfx94x, gfx10xx, gfx11xx, gfx12xx?). \"whl-multi-arch\" — a single wheel containing all arches. Maybe the bundled kernels are missing gfx1151 and only have gfx1150?? But the index's torch page also had an amd-torch-device-gfx1151 metapackage — those amd-torch-device-* packages are per-device wheels! Maybe the generic `torch` wheel doesn't contain gfx1151 kernels, and the device-specific package `amd-torch-device-gfx1151` provides a torch build targeting that device!\n\nThat makes sense: the index root listed amd-torch-device-gfx1151/, amd-torchvision-device-gfx1151/, etc. Let me check the contents of amd-torch-device-gfx1151/.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"text","text":"Even plain float32 kernels fail — suspicious. The index has `amd-torch-device-gfx1151` packages; let me inspect those:\n\n"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_e8c3266c6193423bbac057cf","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"curl -fsSL --max-time 30 \"https://repo.amd.com/rocm/whl-multi-arch/amd-torch-device-gfx1151/\" | grep -oE 'href=\"[^\"]*\"' | grep -i '2.13.0' ; echo ---; curl -fsSL --max-time 30 \"https://repo.amd.com/rocm/whl-multi-arch/amd-torch-device-gfx1151/\" | grep -oE 'href=\"[^\"]*\"' | tail -6","timeout":90}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"qwen-token-plan","model":"qwen3.8-max","usage":{"input":1489,"output":952,"cacheRead":86016,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":759,"totalTokens":88457,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1787986200157,"responseId":"chatcmpl-01941046-88f3-43ac-b627-7946d9bf6a74","rawStopReason":"tool_calls"},"toolResults":[{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_e8c3266c6193423bbac057cf","toolName":"bash","content":[{"type":"text","text":"href=\"../amd_torch_device_gfx1151-2.13.0%2Brocm7.14.0-cp312-cp312-linux_x8