{"type":"message_update","assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"thinking_end","contentIndex":0,"content":"There exists `amd_torch_device_gfx1151-2.13.0+rocm7.14.0-cp312-linux_x86_64.whl` — the device-specific torch wheel! Presumably it replaces/overrides the generic torch with kernels built for gfx1151. And `amd-torchvision-device-gfx1151` likely exists too.\n\nThe generic `torch` wheel under /torch/ is probably multi-arch, but does it not include kernels for gfx1151? \"Multi-arch\" = multiple arches bundled in; but the device-specific ones = tuned builds? Let's grab the METADATA of the amd_torch_device_gfx1151 wheel (a full GET works; is it a small meta wheel? The size might be huge — it could be a full torch replacement). Before downloading GB, let me inspect the metadata with a GET + streaming ZIP read: download just enough… GET works, HEAD/Range are refused. Hmm, downloading a 2.5GB wheel just to check is heavy, but the machine has bandwidth. Alternative: pip download --no-deps into the pip cache dir and inspect. Actually, let's just do pip download --no-deps amd-torch-device-gfx1151==2.13.0+rocm7.14.0 to /tmp and check the METADATA (it'll get fully downloaded anyway).\n\nWait — but what did kyuz0's workflow do? His build-ubuntu-stable auto-detected and used `torch==2.11.0+rocm7.14.0` from the /torch/ index — the generic one. And his images work on Strix Halo (gfx1151)! rocm7.14.0-torch2.11.0-vllm0.28.0 is the standard image working on this fleet. So the generic torch wheel works on gfx1151… on torch 2.11. Could it be that the generic torch 2.13.0 wheel dropped gfx1151 kernels?? And the device-specific wheel is what provides gfx1151 on 2.13?\n\nOr, the host driver might be too old: hipErrorInvalidImage may mean the ISA version of the kernel binary exceeds what the running kernel supports. Check the host's amdgpu version. What's the host's kernel? uname -r. ROCm kernel driver 7.1.4 → \"matches the kernel's 7.1.4 driver.\" Let me check the actual host."}}
