{"type":"message_update","assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"thinking_end","contentIndex":0,"content":"This is the missing piece: `amd-torch-device-gfx1151` ships `torch/.kpack/torch_gfx1151.kpack` — a kernel pack containing precompiled device kernels for gfx1151! And it requires `rocm-sdk-device-gfx1151==7.14.0` (yet another device package). The generic torch wheel apparently lazy-loads device kernels from .kpack — so that's why hipErrorInvalidImage is thrown: kernels for gfx1151 weren't installed.\n\nSo, the complete pinned set = torch, torchvision, triton, rocm-sdk-core (+libraries/bootstrap/rocm), plus the device packages: amd-torch-device-gfx1151, amd-torchvision-device-gfx1151, rocm-sdk-device-gfx1151. Check whether the index has rocm-sdk-device-gfx1151? It wasn't shown in the root listing from earlier… What showed up in the root listing were: apex, filelock, fsspec, jax-*, jinja2, markupsafe, mpmath, networkx, numpy, pillow, rocm, rocm-bootstrap, rocm-profiler, rocm-sdk-core, rocm-sdk-devel, rocm-sdk-libraries, setuptools, sympy, torch, torchaudio, torchvision, triton, typing-extensions. No rocm-sdk-device-* at the root… hmm. Maybe the rocm-sdk-device-gfx1151 wheel exists under a different name, or it's provided from the rocm-sdk-libraries wheel? But the Requires-Dist says rocm-sdk-device-gfx1151==7.14.0 — pip would need to resolve it from the index. Let me re-check the root listing (it might have been truncated at head -40… it was filtered with grep -viE device-gfx! I excluded device-gfx lines, but rocm-sdk-device-gfx1151 also contains \"device-gfx\" → got filtered out!). Let me re-check."}}
{"type":"message_update","assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"text_end","contentIndex":1,"content":"The missing piece: device kernel packs. Let me enumerate all device packages on the index:\n\n"}}
