ture":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"text","text":"GPU is seen but the first kernel launch fails. Let me diagnose:\n\n"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_87b0dce587d8451296b8b46c","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"cd /home/tom/.local/state/tally/campaigns/flashnext/flashnext/824b68c09c23/container-recipe && podman run -i --rm --device /dev/kfd --device /dev/dri --security-opt seccomp=unconfined --ipc=host flashnext:dev python3 - <<'PY'\nimport torch\nprint(torch.cuda.get_device_properties(0))\ntry:\n    y = torch.ones(4, device=\"cuda\") + 1\n    print(\"float32 kernel ok:\", y.cpu().tolist())\nexcept Exception as e:\n    print(\"float32 kernel FAIL:\", e)\ntry:\n    z = torch.zeros(4, dtype=torch.float8_e4m3fn, device=\"cuda\")\n    print(\"fp8 alloc ok\")\nexcept Exception as e:\n    print(\"fp8 alloc FAIL:\", e)\nPY\necho \"exit=$?\"","timeout":300}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"qwen-token-plan","model":"qwen3.8-max","usage":{"input":1677,"output":559,"cacheRead":84992,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":298,"totalTokens":87228,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1787986184807,"responseId":"chatcmpl-bc5489f9-44fe-4b07-b1d9-dff43b617356","rawStopReason":"tool_calls"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_87b0dce587d8451296b8b46c","toolName":"bash","content":[{"type":"text","text":"_CudaDeviceProperties(name='AMD Radeon 8060S Graphics', major=11, minor=5, gcnArchName='gfx1151', total_memory=131072MB, multi_processor_count=20, uuid=58580000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000, pci_bus_id=194, pci_device_id=0, pci_domain_id=0, L2_cache_size=2MB)\nfloat32 kernel FAIL: CUDA error: device kernel image is invalid\nSearch for `hipErrorInvalidImage' in https://rocm.docs.amd.com/projects/HIP/en/latest/index.html for more information.\nFor more detailed error information, run with CUDA_LOG_FILE=stderr\nDevice-side assertion tracking was not enabled by user.\nfp8 alloc FAIL: CUDA error: invalid kernel file\nSearch for `hipErrorInvalidKernelFile' in https://rocm.docs.amd.com/projects/HIP/en/latest/index.html for more information.\nFor more detailed error information, run with CUDA_LOG_FILE=stderr\nDevice-side assertion tracking was not enabled by user.\nexit=0\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1787986200151},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Even a simple float32 kernel fails with hipErrorInvalidImage. The device is detected as gfx1151, and total_memory is 131072MB. In other words, the kernels bundled with the torch wheel don't match this GPU's runtime — probably a kernel driver/userspace mismatch. The host's kernel is on ROCm 7.1.4 driver, and IMPORTS says rocm 7.14.0 userspace \"matches the kernel's 7.1.4 driver on both nodes.\" But this host might have a different kernel/driver, or might need an environment variable like HSA_OVERRIDE_GFX_VERSION=11.5.1 (gfx1151 often requires HSA_OVERRIDE_GFX_VERSION=11.5.0/11.5.1 since the runtime only knows about gfx1150?). For Strix Halo, isn't the known HSA_OVERRIDE_GFX_VERSION 11.5.1? On Strix Point (gfx1150), people use HSA_OVERRIDE_GFX_VERSION=11.5.0. On Strix Halo (gfx1151), the override is also 11.5.1... hmm, wait — but the AMD-provided gfx1151 wheels are supposed to work natively.\n\nWait a sec — is this host really a \"coordinator\" node with the proper amdgpu stack? Check the host's ROCm kernel version: `modinfo amdgpu | grep version` or cat /sys/module/amdgpu/version, and whether a host-level torch works. But hold on: the cp-smoke checkpoint is supposed to run on this estate, and the evidence says the nodes have been validated (the \"2026-08-28 verified\" index is gfx1151-capable). kyuz0's toolboxes run on this fleet...\n\nLet me check the host: does host-level torch with the same wheels work here? Also check for missing environment variables. Try HSA_OVERRIDE_GFX_VERSION=11.5.1:\n\nAlso, hipErrorInvalidImage can occur when the runtime rejects an ISA it doesn't know about: the host kernel's KFD might be reporting gfx1151, but the HSA runtime inside the container might not support it... the rocm-sdk-core 7.14 userspace definitely knows gfx1151.\n\nActually, wait: \"device kernel image is invalid\" often happens when the amdgpu kernel module is older than required by the ISA, or when the code object targets a different gfx. The torch wheels are... the wheel's torch ships binaries compiled for a set of architectures (multi-arch: gfx90x, gfx94x, gfx10xx, gfx11xx, gfx12xx?). \"whl-multi-arch\" — a single wheel containing all arches. Maybe the bundled kernels are missing gfx1151 and only have gfx1150?? But the index's torch page also had an amd-torch-device-gfx1151 metapackage — those amd-torch-device-* packages are per-device wheels! Maybe the generic `torch` wheel doesn't contain gfx1151 kernels, and the device-specific package `amd-torch-device-gfx1151` provides a torch build targeting that device!\n\nThat makes sense: the index root listed amd-torch-device-gfx1151/, amd-torchvision-device-gfx1151/, etc. Let me check the contents of amd-torch-device-gfx1151/.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"text","text":"Even plain float32 kernels fail — suspicious. The index has `amd-torch-device-gfx1151` packages; let me inspect those:\n\n"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_e8c3266c6193423bbac057cf","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"curl -fsSL --max-time 30 \"https://repo.amd.com/rocm/whl-multi-arch/amd-torch-device-gfx1151/\" | grep -oE 'href=\"[^\"]*\"' | grep -i '2.13.0' ; echo ---; curl -fsSL --max-time 30 \"https://repo.amd.com/rocm/whl-multi-arch/amd-torch-device-gfx1151/\" | grep -oE 'href=\"[^\"]*\"' | tail -6","timeout":90}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"qwen-token-plan","model":"qwen3.8-max","usage":{"input":1489,"output":952,"cacheRead":86016,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":759,"totalTokens":88457,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1787986200157,"responseId":"chatcmpl-0194