ot require modifying run-smoke.sh, and its acceptance criteria pass. However, the delivered image will cause cp-smoke to fail due to this mismatch.\n\nOptions: (a) complete the task and flag the issue in the final message (as a note, or as needs-authority). The needs-authority result would block committing the work — the task itself is completable within the boundary; the smoke script issue belongs to a separate lane / operator. Complete the task, commit, and in the final message prominently mention the latent defect in run-smoke.sh (as information, not needs-authority, since my task's completion doesn't require that path). Hmm — but would it be better to return needs-authority to let the operator make the call? The brief says: \"If completion requires paths under the authority surface, return needs-authority.\" My completion does not require it. So: complete and note the finding.\n\nNext, rebuild the image with the device wheel baked in. The rebuild reuses the cache up through the substrate step; the substrate layer changes → re-execute; pip cache mount speeds it up. After that, re-verify that GPU probes pass with the baked-in image, and re-run build.sh as a whole to regenerate the receipt (currently stale — the receipt references the old image digest). Actually simpler: re-run build.sh wholesale (packaging tests + build + receipt). About 15 minutes total.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"text","text":"The fork hook returns a bare `bool` — this is a latent bug in the existing `run-smoke.sh` (outside my scope of responsibility), which I'll point out at the end. Next, I'll rebuild the image with the device wheels baked in, and regenerate the receipt:\n\n"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_a7f0c7cc019a425a9097c2f8","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"cd /home/tom/.local/state/tally/campaigns/flashnext/flashnext/824b68c09c23/container-recipe && rm -f results/receipts/build.json && nohup bash container/build.sh > .build/build.log 2>&1 & echo \"pid=$!\""